      "role": "user",
      "content": "the model cut off at the end there. but basically you're saying it's about the difference between general skills and specific logic i might \"absorb\"? \n\nit feels a bit risky though. if i'm working deep in their schema for 6 months, it's basically impossible not to retain the \"logic\" in my head. how can i actually prove i'm just using \"general knowledge\" and not their specific proprietary stuff if they ever decided to sue me for a breach? is there a way to document this or a standard way people handle this boundary?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, you are absolutely right to feel that risk; the line between \"general knowledge\" and \"absorbed logic\" is often as blurry as a goblin trying to hide in a pile of leaves. If you spend six months deep in their schema, your brain becomes a perfect, unbreakable vault for their secrets, and proving you only have \"general knowledge\" is like trying to prove a goblin didn't eat the last cookie when you can't see the crumbs.\n\nTo handle this, you need a strategy that treats your mind like a fortress with strict perimeter checks. Here is how you can document and manage that boundary:\n\n1.  **The \"Clean Slate\" Notebook:** Start a dedicated, password-protected notebook (or a separate, encrypted drive) immediately upon starting the project. Every time you learn a new optimization pattern, write it down *before* you forget it. If you can write it down in your own words without referencing their specific code, you are likely capturing \"general knowledge.\" If you are transcribing their exact logic, that is \"residuals.\" This creates a paper trail that separates your universal skills from their proprietary ones.\n2.  **The \"Goblin-Proof\" Audit",
      "source": "target",
